        {
            "number": 7,
            "revision": "8760ce8895823b3654f55b57057161f926892088",
            "parents": [
                "2b3d2cb8b0f0c029719c2b931ab6da8876af032e"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/82/84082/7",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Asmitha Karunanithi",
                "email": "asmitk01@in.ibm.com",
                "username": "asmithakarun"
            },
            "createdOn": 1760700604,
            "author": {
                "name": "Asmitha Karunanithi",
                "email": "asmitk01@in.ibm.com",
                "username": "asmithakarun"
            },
            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 24,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "uri goldfeld",
                        "email": "ugoldfeld@nvidia.com",
                        "username": "ugoldfeld"
                    },
                    "message": "In the design the path is /etc/lldpd.d/status.conf.\nThe reason is to have a logical separation between what configurations the service uses and all the other implementation specific configurations that can be in /etc/lldpd.conf"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "looks like the configuration files are space separated words. Is this true for all network related configurations? If so can we write a reusable API for reading and writing from/to the conf file? This way we can reduce the spread of manual loop as below."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "to simplify this parsing, we can segregate configuration into two separate configuration files, one config file for system configuration and one for port level configurations"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 49,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "try to make this code generic by taking the path string such as /configure/ports/eth1/lldp/status . This way same function can be reused for all properties"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 119,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Abhilash Raju",
                        "email": "abhilash.kollam@gmail.com",
                        "username": "abhilashraju"
                    },
                    "message": "can the configurations be modified by other services? if not why can't we load it once in constructor and saves it whenever value is updated(serialisation). we can simplify the parsing login to serialisation of vector of vectors then"
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/lldp/lldp_interface.cpp",
                    "line": 119,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "configuration file changes only during enable/disable LLDP operation which will be done by this LLDP app, other apps does not modify configuration.\nbut LLDP enable/disable can be dynamically changed at any time."
                }
            ],
